# Marker-gap filling: axis and selection are separate hypotheses

A demo that inserts a paint color into single-cell gaps between same-color markers in a horizontal stripe establishes a local pattern for that observed axis. It does not by itself establish that the same interpolation applies vertically, that arbitrary aligned pairs should be completed, or that all cells between the row's extreme markers qualify.

When evaluating sparse repeated markers, compare these candidate masks independently:

- fill each one-cell gap between successive markers;
- fill all background cells between the extreme markers;
- extend markers as rays or crosshairs;
- apply the observed operation along one axis only versus both axes.

Preserve original markers and untouched regions unless the complete demos show otherwise. A hypothesis must explain all changed and unchanged cells in every pair. After rejection, discard the whole prediction and request a discriminating example when candidate outputs differ. Never infer a two-axis rule solely by rotating the interpretation of a single-axis demonstration.

Before submission, run the exact task transform on literal demo fixtures and the test. Verify fixture row counts and widths as well as complete matrix equality; successful code execution on a manually mis-sized grid is not evidence.

# Periodic lattice: evidence ledger and recovery

This session had an existing program that directly sampled a lattice; its first output was rejected. Five subsequent examples varied lattice pitch, palette, orientation, and output dimensions, but no complete machine-readable fixtures were assembled and no single rule was validated across them. Another speculative sampling transform was then submitted and rejected. The durable lesson is procedural: do not infer a universal sampling rule from one matching visual motif or output dimensions.

## Reusable checklist

For each complete pair, record:
- input/output dimensions and palette roles;
- exact horizontal and vertical separator coordinates and periods, separately;
- phase/origin and edge truncation;
- all colored junctions or guide marks;
- full output, not only shape or a few sampled values.

Keep candidate mappings distinct: junction sampling, interval/tile aggregation, guide-line marker sequence, and panel/background encoding. For each, predict every output cell in every demo and the test. Reject a candidate if it needs an unsupported phase/crop/color special case or does not explain ordinary fill symbols and omitted boundaries.

Only promote an implementation after storing literal fixtures and obtaining exact full-matrix equality on every pair from the task's on-disk `transform`. Preserve an older verified transform during exploration. Following a wrong verdict, reset the mapping as a whole; do not make local stride/offset tweaks without demo evidence. If hypotheses still imply different outputs, use a targeted demo request rather than spending an attempt on a plausible grid.

